---
import Mark from "../components/Mark.astro"
import { site, social, strands } from "../lib/site"
import Root from "../layouts/Root.astro"

const now = new Date()
const stamp = `${now.getFullYear()}-${String(now.getMonth() + 1).padStart(2, "0")}-${String(now.getDate()).padStart(2, "0")}-${String(now.getHours()).padStart(2, "0")}${String(now.getMinutes()).padStart(2, "0")}`
const cvDownloadName = `alex-boffey-cv-${stamp}.pdf`

const channels = [
  { href: `mailto:${site.email}`, label: "Email", value: site.email },
  { href: social.linkedin, label: "LinkedIn", value: "in/alexboffey" },
  { href: social.github, label: "GitHub", value: "alexboffey" },
]
---

<Root title="About" description={site.description} quiet scene="about">
  <div class="about shell">
    <header class="about__head">
      <h1 class="about__title">
        Product engineer,<br />
        <span class="about__accent">with deep frontend roots</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="lead about__lead">
        Hi, I'm Alex. Software engineer in London, nine years in. I started on
        the frontend and it's still where I'm strongest. The work I enjoy most
        is owning a feature the whole way: working out the problem, then the
        API, the data model and the UI.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="about__grid">
      <section class="about__col prose" aria-labelledby="how">
        <h2 id="how">How I work</h2>
        <p>
          I like the part of the job where an intent meets what the machine will
          actually do, and someone has to decide which one moves. Usually that
          means building the thing properly once so forty features do not each
          solve it badly, whether that thing is a component or a service.
        </p>
        <p>
          Simple, small, contained, easy to change beats complex and
          over-engineered every time. Code that does one thing is easy to
          change; code that does seven is not. You will have to change it next
          month, and that is the whole case for keeping it small.
        </p>
        <p>
          CI does the checks. Let engineers work the way they work; enforce at
          the PR rather than at save-time or on every commit. A gate that is one
          thing in one place is a gate people trust.
        </p>
        <p>
          The e2e tests worth having drive the full system the way a real user
          would. Happy-path assertions are easy to write and often add little. A
          green suite can be entirely consistent with a broken feature.
        </p>
        <p>
          Accessibility and performance are not a later pass. They are cheaper
          at the primitive than at the feature, which is the entire argument for
          having a system in the first place.
        </p>

        <h2>The three strands</h2>
        <dl class="about__strands">
          {
            strands.map((strand) => (
              <>
                <dt class="about__strand-name">{strand.name}</dt>
                <dd class="about__strand-body">{strand.summary}</dd>
              </>
            ))
          }
        </dl>

        <h2>Music &amp; audio</h2>
        <p>
          Lifelong guitar player, mostly electric and I love effects, with some
          classical on the side.
        </p>
        <p>
          I play in a band and own the technical side of it: demos, sound
          design, synthesis, sampling, and the live rig. The parts that need an
          audio engineer get one. I collaborate rather than fake it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The live rig is a hardware setup that takes production tracks and
          breaks them down into a form the band can play against: stems,
          playable effects, and a cue system that runs the show. On the side I
          have been experimenting with MIDI-synced visuals through OBS driven
          off the same hardware, and with sequencers and set-prep utilities in
          the production workflow.
        </p>
        <p>
          The two sides inform each other, mostly in one direction: software
          habits travel into the music tech. Don't trust it until it is tested.
          Use compute efficiently. Keep it simple.
        </p>
      </section>

      <aside class="about__side" id="contact">
        <div class="about__card">
          <Mark size={40} />
          <h2 class="about__card-title">Get in touch</h2>
          <p class="about__card-body">
            If you are hiring, or you want to argue about design tokens, email
            is the fastest way to reach me.
          </p>

          <dl class="channels">
            {
              channels.map((channel) => (
                <div class="channel">
                  <dt class="channel__label">{channel.label}</dt>
                  <dd class="channel__value">
                    <a
                      href={channel.href}
                      rel={
                        channel.href.startsWith("http")
                          ? "me noopener"
                          : undefined
                      }
                    >
                      {channel.value}
                    </a>
                  </dd>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </dl>

          <div class="about__ctas">
            <a class="action action--primary" href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>
              Email me
            </a>
            <a
              class="action"
              href="/alex-boffey-cv.pdf"
              download={cvDownloadName}
            >
              Download CV
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</Root>

<style>
  .about {
    padding-block: var(--space-2xl) var(--space-3xl);
  }

  .about__head {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-xl);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .about__title {
    font-size: var(--size-display);
    font-stretch: var(--wdth-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 0.96;
    color: var(--paper);
  }

  .about__accent {
    color: var(--incandescent);
  }

  .about__lead {
    max-width: 54ch;
  }

  .about__lead a {
    color: var(--incandescent);
  }

  .about__grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2xl);
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
  }

  @media (min-width: 62rem) {
    .about__grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(18rem, 24rem);
      gap: var(--space-3xl);
      align-items: start;
    }

    .about__side {
      position: sticky;
      top: var(--space-lg);
    }
  }

  .about__strands {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
  }

  .about__strand-name {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--size-label);
    font-stretch: 88%;
    letter-spacing: var(--track-label);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--incandescent);
  }

  .about__strand-body {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  /* Not a card. A hairline above, the mark, and the channel rows sitting on the
     page's own ground: the contract says no cards anywhere and meant it. */
  .about__card {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    padding-top: var(--space-lg);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--incandescent);
  }

  .about__card-title {
    font-size: var(--size-sub);
    font-stretch: var(--wdth-title);
    color: var(--paper);
  }

  .about__card-body {
    font-size: var(--size-small);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  .channels {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0;
    margin: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .channel {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    padding-block: var(--space-sm);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .channel__label {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--size-micro);
    font-stretch: 88%;
    letter-spacing: var(--track-label);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-label);
  }

  .channel__value {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--size-small);
    text-align: right;
  }

  .channel__value a {
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--paper);
  }

  .channel__value a:hover,
  .channel__value a:focus-visible {
    color: var(--incandescent);
  }

  .about__ctas {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    justify-self: start;
  }
</style>
